Title: Stack Exchange about the opinion: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

### Why does the Federal Circuit have jurisdiction over this TTAB appeal, and why are C.C.P.A. cases like DuPont binding?
Question:
The opinion cites 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B) for jurisdiction and relies heavily on C.C.P.A. precedent such as DuPont. Can someone explain the statutory path that routes TTAB appeals to the Federal Circuit (as opposed to regional circuits), and why pre-1982 C.C.P.A. trademark decisions are treated as binding on the Federal Circuit?
Answer:
Short answer
- Jurisdiction: The Federal Circuit heard this appeal because it is an appeal from a Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) decision and the court expressly invoked 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B) as its statutory basis for jurisdiction (Opinion, I). That is the route that channels TTAB/PTO appeals to the Federal Circuit.
- C.C.P.A. precedents like DuPont are treated as binding authority for TTAB appeals because the Federal Circuit relies on the established body of PTO/TTAB law developed by the C.C.P.A.; the opinion shows this by using Application of E.I. DuPont DeNemours & Co., 476 F.2d 1357 (C.C.P.A. 1973) as the governing statement of the likelihood-of-confusion standard (Opinion, II).

Why that matters (short explanation of the doctrinal posture)
- Statutory route: The opinion begins by stating jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B). That statute is the court’s cited hook for appellate review of USPTO/TTAB decisions; accordingly the Federal Circuit is the appellate forum for TTAB decisions in this case (Opinion, I).
- Governing legal standard: The DuPont decision (cited as a C.C.P.A. case in the opinion) supplies the multi‑factor likelihood‑of‑confusion test the Board and this court apply. The opinion treats DuPont as the controlling statement of law: “A trademark opposition under Section 2(d) of the Lanham Act requires registration refusal when ‘confusion is likely…’ Application of E.I. DuPont DeNemours & Co., 476 F.2d 1357, 1360 (C.C.P.A. 1973)” (Opinion, II).
- Role of precedent and review standards: The opinion makes clear how the Federal Circuit handles TTAB appeals: legal conclusions are reviewed de novo and factual findings (including findings under each DuPont factor) are tested for substantial evidence. The court therefore applies DuPont as the legal standard and examines the Board’s factual findings against the substantial‑evidence standard (Opinion, II; see also citations to M2 Software and Bose in Opinion, II).

Putting it together
- Because the Federal Circuit is the statutorily designated appellate forum for PTO/TTAB decisions in cases of this type (the court cites 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B)), the court applies the body of law that governs TTAB adjudications. DuPont is part of that body of law (it is the canonical statement of the likelihood‑of‑confusion factors for TTAB matters), so the Federal Circuit treats DuPont and like C.C.P.A. decisions as the controlling precedent when resolving TTAB appeals — which is exactly what the opinion does throughout its analysis (Opinion, II–III).

### What standing is required to appeal a TTAB opposition decision to the Federal Circuit, and why didn't the court address it here?
Question:
The opinion does not discuss Article III standing. In a direct appeal under 15 U.S.C. § 1071(a), what injury-in-fact must a trademark applicant show to have standing in the Federal Circuit? Is being the party whose registration was refused per se sufficient, and is there any difference compared to standing requirements for TTAB oppositions or district court actions under § 1071(b)?
Answer:
Short answer
The Federal Circuit did not discuss Article III standing in Apex Bank v. CC Serve because standing was not contested and was facially satisfied by Apex’s status as the applicant whose registration was refused. The court invoked statutory appellate jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B) and proceeded to decide the merits. In a direct appeal under 15 U.S.C. § 1071(a) the usual Article III requirements apply: the appellant must show an injury‑in‑fact that is concrete and particularized, traceable to the Board’s decision, and redressable by the court. Being the party whose registration was refused normally supplies that injury‑in‑fact (loss of the statutory right to register) and therefore is ordinarily sufficient to confer Article III standing in a § 1071(a) appeal. The standing inquiry for oppositions before the TTAB or for district‑court actions under § 1071(b) is governed by different procedural routes (and the TTAB has its own threshold “interest/damage” requirements for opposers), but Article III’s core injury/causation/redressability elements still govern federal court suits.

Explanation and support from the opinion
- The opinion opens by describing Apex as the applicant whose registration the Board refused and then states the court’s appellate jurisdiction: “We have jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1295(a)(4)(B).” (Opinion, § I.) The court never flagged standing as an issue and addressed the merits. That combination strongly indicates the court treated standing as satisfied or uncontested and therefore unnecessary to resolve.
- Constitutional standing doctrine (Article III) requires an injury‑in‑fact that is concrete and particularized, causation (the injury is fairly traceable to the defendant’s action), and redressability (a favorable decision will likely remedy the injury). In the TTAB/registration context, an adverse Board refusal plainly inflicts a concrete, particularized injury on the applicant: the denial of the statutory privilege of registration and the legal consequences that attend registration. Reversal of the Board would redress that injury by permitting registration. Thus, in a direct § 1071(a) appeal the refused‑applicant’s status typically satisfies Article III.
- Why the court didn’t separately analyze standing here: the opinion shows the procedural posture (applicant appeals adverse Board decision) and then proceeds to review the merits; it neither disclaims jurisdiction nor explains any Article III defect. That is consistent with appellate practice: courts will ordinarily omit a standing discussion when the party invoking review is plainly the directly injured party and no party contests standing.

Brief note on differences with TTAB oppositions and § 1071(b) district actions
- TTAB oppositions are regulated proceedings with their own threshold statutory/tribunal requirements for who may oppose (the TTAB asks whether an opposer has the requisite interest and reasonable grounds to oppose). Those are agency/tribunal standing rules distinct from Article III, and a party who can litigate before the TTAB is not automatically guaranteed Article III standing in federal court.
- A § 1071(b) action (civil action in district court) is a federal court suit and therefore must satisfy Article III’s injury/causation/redressability elements. The practical injury for a party suing in district court will often mirror the injury in an appeal (denial or threat to registration or market position), but the forum (district court instead of direct appeal) changes procedural posture and which legal standards and remedies are invoked.

Bottom line
Apex’s appeal was a garden‑variety § 1071(a) direct appeal by the aggrieved applicant whose registration was refused; the refusal itself is the paradigmatic injury‑in‑fact. Because standing was obvious and uncontested, the Federal Circuit invoked § 1295 jurisdiction and reached the merits without a freestanding Article III discussion.

### Why did the court vacate and remand instead of reweighing the DuPont factors itself?
Question:
The panel found legal error in the Board’s sixth-factor analysis and vacated the first factor because commercial impression could change if strength changes. Given that likelihood of confusion is a question of law, why didn’t the Federal Circuit reweigh the factors and decide the § 2(d) issue? Is this because factual findings under each factor are for the Board and reviewed for substantial evidence (and/or because of Chenery-type limits on appellate factfinding for agencies)?
Answer:
Short answer: Because the panel corrected a legal error in the Board’s analysis that requires the Board — not the court — to reapply the correct legal standard to the factual record and make any necessary factual findings. The Federal Circuit reviews the Board’s legal conclusions de novo but reviews the underlying DuPont fact-findings for substantial evidence; it will not usurp the Board by making new fact-findings or reweighing evidence in the first instance.

Why that follows from the opinion
- The court framed the doctrine up front: “Likelihood of confusion is a question of law, based on findings of relevant underlying facts, namely findings under the DuPont factors,” and “[e]ach of the [thirteen] DuPont factors presents a question of fact, findings with regard to which we test for substantial evidence.” (Opinion §II (citing M2 Software; Bose)). That establishes the appellate role: correct legal error; review factual findings for substantial evidence; do not ordinarily make new factual findings.
- On the sixth factor the Board made a legal error in defining the universe of third‑party marks it could consider (it limited the universe to “credit card services” even though the Board had already found the parties’ services to be highly similar). The court said that was a legal mistake and therefore “we vacate the Board’s finding with respect to the sixth DuPont factor and remand for reconsideration of the appropriate scope of third‑party marks.” (Opinion §III.B). Because the Board applied the wrong legal standard, the court could not simply accept the Board’s factual conclusion as properly framed.
- The court also vacated the first factor because commercial impression “informs the analysis under the first DuPont factor,” and a corrected sixth‑factor analysis “may result in a different determination of the mark’s commercial strength or weakness and affect the overall commercial impression.” (Opinion §III.C). That shows the court recognized interdependence between fact‑intensive DuPont factors and that a remand is necessary where a legal error in one factor could change factual conclusions under another.

Relation to Chenery / limits on appellate factfinding
- The opinion follows the same separation-of-functions principle reflected in Chenery: an agency must apply the correct legal standard and make the necessary factual findings; a reviewing court should not supply missing factual determinations or reweigh the evidence in the first instance. The Federal Circuit therefore corrected the legal error and remanded so the Board can reapply the correct legal standard to the record and make any attendant factual findings (subject to appellate substantial‑evidence review).

Practical takeaway
- Where the Board misstates the legal standard that determines what evidence is relevant (here, the scope of third‑party marks), the Federal Circuit will vacate and remand rather than reweigh the DuPont factors itself, because the remaining determinations are factual and properly belong to the Board to make under the corrected legal standard.

### What is the legal justification for requiring a consistent scope of “similar goods” across DuPont factors two and six?
Question:
The court faults the Board for defining similarity broadly under factor two (services are highly similar/legally identical in part) but narrowly under factor six (only credit card services). Is there a doctrinal rule that the scope of “similar goods/services” must be consistent across factors, or is the court creating a harmonization principle here? How should the Board determine the correct breadth of the relevant marketplace for third-party-use evidence post–Apex Bank?
Answer:
Short answer
The Court’s holding in Apex Bank is not inventing a new substantive test for DuPont factor six; it is insisting on reasoned consistency between the Board’s factual findings. Because factor six only requires “similar” (not identical) goods/services, once the Board has already found the parties’ services to be highly similar (indeed “legally identical, in part”) under factor two, it must use that same scope when deciding which third‑party marks count under factor six—unless it gives a reasoned, record‑based explanation for departing from that scope. Apex Bank thus enforces a harmonization/consistency principle grounded in the existing DuPont precedent the Board already applies.

Why the Court says this
- DuPont factor two asks whether the parties’ goods/services are related; factor six asks whether there are many similar marks in use on similar goods/services. The Court recalled that factor six “does not require identical goods—only similar ones” (citing Olde Tyme Foods and Juice Generation). Apex Bank, slip op. § III.B.
- The Board found the parties’ services “highly similar” (even partially “legally identical”) under factor two. The Court held it was error for the Board then to restrict the universe of third‑party marks considered under factor six to a narrower category (credit‑card services only), saying “the Board should retain the same scope in its consideration of similarity under the other factors.” Apex Bank, slip op. § III.B (emphasis supplied).
- The Court framed this as an error of analysis, not a new substantive limitation: “We see no reason to impose a different and more stringent legal standard for similarity under the sixth DuPont factor.” Id.

What this means doctrinally
- There is no freestanding rule that every DuPont factor must use the exact same phrasing in every case. Rather, Apex Bank requires internal consistency: factual findings about what services are “similar” (and the marketplace/consumer class that follows from that finding) should inform which third‑party uses are “similar” for factor six.
- Put differently: factor six still asks about marks on “similar goods/services,” but the Board cannot bootstrap a broad similarity finding under factor two and then narrow the relevant universe under factor six without explaining why the narrower definition is supported by the record.

How the Board should approach the scope of the relevant marketplace for third‑party‑use evidence post‑Apex Bank
Follow a two‑step, record‑based approach:

1) Fix the baseline similarity/factual finding under factor two.
   - Use the pleaded/identified services, dictionary/identifications, third‑party registrations, channels of trade, classes of customers, and marketing evidence to determine how broadly the services are related (as the Board did when it found banking/finance services encompass credit‑card activity). Apex Bank, § III.A.

2) Use that baseline to define which third‑party marks are “on similar goods/services” for factor six.
   - Include third‑party uses and registrations that fall within the scope of similarity established under factor two (the Court said the Board “should retain the same scope” when considering factor six). Apex Bank, § III.B.
   - Analyze those third‑party examples to assess commercial strength (how many, how widespread, how prominent), remembering that factor six does not require identical goods—only similarity (Olde Tyme Foods; Juice Generation). Apex Bank, § III.B.
   - If the Board wants to consider a narrower universe than the factor‑two finding would suggest, it must explain why the record supports a different scope (for example, evidence that consumers treat a subset of services as distinct, distinct channels of trade, different classes of buyers, or other record evidence justifying a narrower marketplace).

Practical consequences
- A Board finding that services are “highly similar” (or “partially identical”) will ordinarily require the Board to consider third‑party marks across that same breadth when evaluating factor six.
- Reconsideration of factor six may change the commercial‑strength analysis and therefore may require reexamination of factor one (as the Court ordered here). Apex Bank, § III.C.

Bottom line
Apex Bank enforces internal consistency across DuPont factors: once the Board determines the relevant scope of similarity under factor two, it should carry that scope forward to factor six (and explain any departure). This is a harmonization requirement grounded in existing precedent that factor six looks to “similar” goods/services—not a new, more demanding rule.

### How should evidence of third-party marks be weighed: does marketplace ‘use’ matter more than ‘registration’?
Question:
The opinion cites Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin, which looked at volumes of third-party uses/registrations to show weakness. The Board here discounted 42 non–credit card uses and only credited 9 credit-card uses. On remand, what kinds of third-party evidence (registrations, websites, advertising, consumer exposure metrics) are most probative of conceptual vs. commercial weakness, and is there any principled threshold for what counts as a “considerable” number?
Answer:
Short answer
- The Sixth DuPont factor asks whether a field is crowded with similar marks; both registrations and evidence of actual marketplace use can show weakness, but they are not interchangeable. Registrations tend to show conceptual (relative) weakness; actual use (websites, ads, sales, consumer exposure metrics) is far more probative of commercial weakness — i.e., that consumers have been trained to distinguish similar marks.  
- Apex requires the Board on remand to count third‑party marks across the same scope of services it found under the Second DuPont factor (banking/financial services broadly), not just credit‑card labels. See Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., slip op. at III.B (vacating Board’s narrow scope).  
- There is no fixed numerical cutoff for what is “considerable.” Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin are examples where courts found 26 and a comparable “voluminous” set persuasive; but whether a number is “considerable” depends on the marketplace, similarity of goods/services, and quality of evidence (use v. mere registration, consumer exposure).

Explanation and guidance for the remand analysis

1) Legal framework (as the opinion applies it)
- The Sixth DuPont factor looks to “the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods” to assess whether a mark is relatively weak and entitled to a narrow scope of protection. Apex cites Palm Bay, Juice Generation, and Jack Wolfskin for this point. Apex Bank, slip op. § III.B (citing Palm Bay Imps., Juice Gen., Jack Wolfskin).  
- Where the Board has already found the parties’ services to be highly similar (here, partially legally identical banking/finance services), it must use that same scope when identifying third‑party marks to consider under the Sixth factor. Apex Bank, slip op. at III.B (vacating because Board restricted universe to credit‑card uses only).

2) Types of third‑party evidence and what they prove
- Registrations (USPTO registrations or applications) — primarily show conceptual/commonness of a term or element across the field. Multiple third‑party registrations using the same formative (e.g., “Aspire”) support a finding that the word is relatively weak as a source identifier for the field (conceptual weakness). The Board and courts have treated registrational evidence as relevant (see Jack Wolfskin and Juice Generation cited in Apex).  
- Actual marketplace use (websites, specimens, advertising, sales materials, contracts, screenshots, trade press) — these show commercial strength or weakness: how frequently consumers actually encounter the term, who uses it in practice, whether channels overlap, and whether consumers have been “educated” to distinguish similar marks. Apex and the cited authorities emphasize that use (not just registration) is persuasive of consumer exposure and thus of a narrowed scope. See Apex Bank, slip op. § III.B (discussing use versus registration in context of Jack Wolfskin/Juice Gen.).  
- Consumer‑facing metrics (search volume, website traffic, ad impressions, sales figures, geographic distribution) — these can be powerful evidence of consumer exposure and marketplace prominence, and thus of commercial strength (or the opposite, if many active competitors dilute distinctiveness). But their probative value depends on linking the metrics to the relevant consumer universe and time period. Apex requires treating the relevant consumer class consistently with the Second factor finding (consumers of similar banking/finance services). Apex Bank, slip op. § III.B.

3) How the Board should weigh these categories on remand
- Use the same scope for third‑party mark collection as the Second factor: because the Board found the services highly similar, include marks used for banking and finance generally (not only narrow “credit card” listings). Apex Bank, slip op. at III.B.  
- Differentiate registrations from use: registrations alone have less weight than registrations plus evidence of use. If a registration is accompanied by evidence that the registrant actually uses the mark in the marketplace (web pages, specimens, sales), it should be given greater weight (as in Jack Wolfskin and Juice Generation, which relied on voluminous registration/use). Apex Bank, slip op. § III.B.  
- Look to consumer exposure/channel overlap: evidence that consumers in the relevant market actually encounter multiple “Aspire” marks (ads, card offerings, bank websites, joint marketing, media coverage) supports a finding that consumers are trained to distinguish and that the mark is commercially weak.  
- Consider qualitative aspects: geographic spread, length of use, prominence in the relevant channels, degree of similarity of the third‑party marks to the marks at issue, and whether the third parties operate in the same channels (online, branch networks, co‑branding with banks) will all affect weight.

4) No strict numerical threshold — context matters
- The Apex opinion rejects the Board’s paring down of the universe rather than endorsing a numeric test. The opinion cites Juice Generation’s characterization of 26 third‑party marks as “a considerable number,” and Jack Wolfskin’s discussion of “voluminous evidence” and 14 notable examples, but does not establish any bright‑line count. Apex Bank, slip op. at III.B (citing Juice Gen., Jack Wolfskin).  
- Practically, the Board should explain why the number and nature of third‑party marks it finds are (or are not) sufficient to show weakness, tying the analysis to (a) the breadth of the market for the parties’ services, (b) whether those third‑party marks are actually used and seen by the relevant consumers, and (c) how many such marks are needed to support a conclusion given the field’s size and concentration. In short: quantify if useful, but justify qualitatively.

5) What to expect on remand
- The Board must reassess the Sixth factor using the broader universe of banking/finance marks. It should catalogue registrations and actual uses, separately note registrations with no evidence of use, and explain how many and what kinds of uses support (or do not support) a finding of relative weakness. Because the First factor (commercial impression) was tied to the Sixth, the Board should revisit that factor after reweighing third‑party evidence. Apex Bank, slip op. at III.C.

Bottom line
On remand the Board should (1) consider third‑party marks across the same, broader service scope it already found similar, (2) treat registrations and marketplace use differently — giving greater weight to evidence of actual consumer exposure (websites, ads, sales, metrics), and (3) decline to apply any bright‑line numerical cutoff; instead explain why the quantity and quality of the third‑party marks it finds are sufficient (or insufficient) to show conceptual or commercial weakness in context. The Apex opinion instructs the Board precisely to correct its overly narrow universe and then reweigh the Sixth (and, in consequence, the First) DuPont factors.

### Was it proper for the Board to interpret the legacy identification “credit card services” using dictionaries after the ID Manual entry was deleted?
Question:
Because the Trademark ID Manual entry for “credit card services” was deleted after CC Serve’s 1998 registration, the Board resorted to dictionary sources to define the scope and then found Apex’s services legally identical in part. Is there a standard for construing the scope of legacy identifications—e.g., based on the identification’s ordinary meaning at the time of registration, current meanings, PTO guidance, or extrinsic evidence? Did the court implicitly endorse the Board’s interpretive method by affirming factor two?
Answer:
Short answer
Yes — the Board’s use of dictionary definitions (and related evidence) to construe a deleted Trademark ID Manual entry was permissible in this case, and the Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s DuPont factor two finding because that construction was supported by substantial evidence. But the court did not announce a categorical rule about which sources (contemporaneous vs. current meanings, PTO guidance, extrinsic evidence) must control when construing “legacy” identifications; its decision is fact‑specific and rests on the Board’s explanation and the evidentiary record.

Why (and what standard the court applied)
- DuPont/factual-legal framework. The likelihood-of-confusion inquiry is legal, based on factual findings under the DuPont factors. Factual findings by the Board are reviewed for substantial evidence; legal conclusions are reviewed de novo. (Opinion §II; citing M2 Software, Consol. Edison.) 
- How the Board construed the ID here. The Board confronted a deleted Trademark ID Manual entry for “credit card services” and “first determined the meaning of the identified services,” relying on dictionary definitions and other evidence to conclude that “credit card services” included issuance, processing, account management, counseling, monitoring, etc. (Op. at J.A. 25–28.) The Board then compared that scope to Apex’s “banking and financing services” and concluded the services were “legally identical, in part.” (J.A. 27–28.)
- Why the court affirmed factor two. The Federal Circuit affirmed because “the Board carefully considered the descriptions of each party’s services, and substantial evidence supports the Board’s finding that the parties’ services are highly similar.” (Op. at §III.A, citing J.A. 27–28 and the Board’s analysis.) In short, the court treated the Board’s construction as a factual finding supported by substantial evidence and accepted the resulting similarity conclusion.

What the opinion does and does not hold about methodology
- What it endorses (implicitly): In this case, using dictionaries and related evidence to give meaning to a deleted ID entry was an acceptable fact-finding method so long as the Board explains its reasoning and the record supports the finding. The court’s affirmation of factor two operates as deference to the Board’s factbound interpretation and evidentiary choices.
- What it does not endorse: The court did not adopt a general rule that dictionary definitions always control, nor did it say whether the controlling meaning must be the ordinary meaning at the time of registration or the current meaning. The opinion is silent on a temporal rule and does not prioritize a particular source (ID Manual, contemporaneous PTO guidance, dictionaries, or extrinsic evidence) as the exclusive correct approach.
- Consistency constraint the court enforced: The court faulted the Board elsewhere for being inconsistent — it found the Board’s narrowing of the universe of third‑party marks under DuPont factor six to be legally flawed because the Board had already found the services “highly similar” under factor two and then limited consideration to only credit‑card services. That underscores that whatever interpretive method the Board uses, it must apply a consistent scope across the DuPont analysis. (See §III.B, J.A. 47–51.)

Practical takeaway for parsing legacy IDs
- The Board may rely on dictionaries, PTO records (including the ID Manual when available), third‑party registrations, and other extrinsic evidence to construe a deleted or otherwise ambiguous identification — but it must (1) explain its reasoning, (2) ground its construction in the record, and (3) apply that construction consistently across DuPont factors. The Federal Circuit will accept that approach when the Board’s findings are supported by substantial evidence, but it will not bless an inconsistent or unexplained methodology.

Bottom line
The court affirmed the Board’s factor two finding because the Board’s dictionary‑based construction of “credit card services” was reasonable and supported by the record. That affirmation is case‑specific deference to the Board’s factual findings; it is not a precedential rule that dictionaries must always be used or that meanings must be fixed at the time of registration.

### Who bears the burden on the sixth DuPont factor to prove a mark’s strength or weakness?
Question:
The Board said CC Serve had not shown particular commercial strength, but also that Apex had not shown the mark was weak, and thus gave ASPIRE the “normal” scope of protection. Is it correct to treat the absence of proof either way as defaulting to normal strength? Do Federal Circuit cases assign a burden of production/persuasion on the opposer or applicant regarding marketplace weakness under factor six?
Answer:
Short answer
The Federal Circuit in Apex did not adopt a special, litigant‑shifting rule for DuPont factor six. The opinion does not say that either party has the ultimate legal burden to prove marketplace weakness; instead, the Board must evaluate the evidence of third‑party use/registration (the “number and nature of similar marks”) against the properly defined scope of goods/services. If a party wants the mark narrowly construed as weak, it must produce the persuasive third‑party evidence; if neither party presents such evidence the Board may (factually) treat the mark as entitled to a normal scope of protection — but only after analyzing the correct universe of comparable uses. See Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., Section III.B (J.A. 47, 51–52).

What the opinion says about burdens and how to apply factor six
- DuPont factor six asks the trier of fact to consider “the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods,” and third‑party use is relevant to showing weakness. Apex cites Palm Bay, Juice Generation, and Jack Wolfskin for this principle (J.A. 46–47).  
- The court treats each DuPont factor as a question of fact (DuPont is a fact‑intensive inquiry) and reviews the Board’s factual findings for substantial evidence (see Part II and DuPont discussion). The Apex opinion does not reallocate a different legal burden of persuasion for factor six; it reviews whether the Board correctly considered the relevant evidence and applied the correct scope of “similar goods/services.” (See J.A. 27–28, 47–48, 51–52.)
- The Board in this case found “CC Serve has not shown that its mark has any particular commercial strength, but [Apex] has not shown that [CC Serve]’s mark is commercially or conceptually weak” and therefore gave the mark the “normal scope of protection” (J.A. 51). The Federal Circuit did not reverse that approach as a per se error; it vacated the Board’s factor‑six finding only because the Board improperly narrowed the universe of third‑party uses to credit‑card services despite having found the parties’ services “highly similar” (and partially legally identical) in its factor‑two analysis (J.A. 27–28, 47–49). The court remanded for reconsideration of factor six with the correct scope of similar goods/services (J.A. 49–52).

Practical rule of thumb (from Apex)
- There is no magic presumption that “no evidence = narrow protection” or vice versa created by Apex. Rather:
  - The party asserting marketplace weakness should present robust third‑party evidence (registrations and evidence of use) covering the properly defined class of goods/services. The court noted that a “considerable” number and nature of such marks (e.g., Juice Generation’s many examples or Jack Wolfskin’s voluminous evidence) support a finding of weakness (J.A. 47–48).  
  - If neither party presents convincing third‑party evidence, the Board may reasonably conclude the mark is entitled to its normal scope — but that conclusion must rest on a correct definition of the relevant market and substantial evidence. Apex vacated the Board’s weight on factor six not because the Board adopted “normal scope” when evidence was sparse, but because the Board applied the wrong universe of comparable uses (J.A. 49–52).

Bottom line
Apex does not reassign a formal burden of persuasion to one side for factor six. The practical burden of producing evidence of weakness falls on the party who wishes to show weakness (typically the later user/applicant), and the Board must weigh whatever third‑party evidence exists against the properly defined scope of goods/services. If the Board errs in defining that scope or ignores relevant third‑party uses, the Federal Circuit will remand for reconsideration (as it did here).

### Why did the court vacate the first DuPont factor based on potential changes to commercial impression from the sixth factor?
Question:
The panel ties commercial impression under the first factor to the outcome of the sixth (strength/weakness). Is this linkage doctrinally required? Under what circumstances does evidence of third-party use alter the perceived commercial impression of an otherwise similar mark, and how should the Board articulate that interplay on remand?
Answer:
Short answer
The panel vacated the first DuPont factor because its treatment of the sixth factor (third‑party use) could change the mark’s commercial strength, and commercial strength is part of the “commercial impression” inquiry under the first factor. The linkage is not a rule that the first factor must always be re‑decided whenever the sixth is; rather, it follows sound DuPont doctrine: evidence that a field is “crowded” (or not) affects how distinctively a mark reads to consumers, which in turn can change the weight and result of the first‑factor comparison. Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., No. 2023‑2143 (Fed. Cir. Sept. 25, 2025) explains that point and remands the Board to reassess both factors consistently.

Why the court vacated the first factor
- The Sixth DuPont factor asks whether there are “a number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods,” and that evidence is probative of a mark’s commercial strength (weakness or strength). As the opinion explains, “Evidence of third‑party use of similar marks on similar goods is relevant to show that a mark is relatively weak and entitled to only a narrow scope of protection.” (Opinion § III.B (citing Palm Bay, Juice Generation, Jack Wolfskin).)
- The First DuPont factor asks for the marks’ “appearance, sound, connotation and commercial impression.” The court tied the two because commercial strength (what consumers have been trained to expect and distinguish) is an element of commercial impression. The panel put it plainly: “Because commercial impression informs the analysis under the first DuPont factor, we must similarly vacate the Board’s analysis here because reconsideration of the sixth DuPont factor may result in a different determination of the mark’s commercial strength or weakness and affect the overall commercial impression.” (Opinion § III.C.)
- Put simply: if reconsideration of third‑party use shows the mark is weaker (or stronger) than the Board found, that changes how the mark is perceived in the marketplace and thus can change the first‑factor outcome—so both must be re‑examined consistently on remand.

Is the linkage doctrinally required?
- There is no mechanical rule that whenever the sixth factor is reweighed the first factor must automatically be vacated. But doctrinally, it is proper and often necessary to revisit the first factor when the evidence bearing on the mark’s strength changes. The court invoked established precedent that third‑party use bears directly on strength and consumer sophistication/education (Palm Bay, Juice Generation, Jack Wolfskin), and that commercial impression is what the first factor measures (DuPont, I.AM.Symbolic). When the Board’s sixth‑factor analysis is materially altered, the Board should reassess whether that altered view of strength changes its view of commercial impression and likelihood of confusion.

When third‑party use alters commercial impression (practical guide drawn from the opinion)
- Scope overlap: The third‑party uses must be in the same relevant field or services the Board has found to be similar. The opinion faulted the Board for narrowing the universe to only “credit card services” after it had already found the parties’ services “highly similar” (partially legally identical) in banking/finance. The Board should use the same scope it adopted in factor two when deciding which third‑party marks are “similar.” (Opinion § III.B.)
- Quantity and quality of uses: A few isolated third‑party uses (the Board here counted nine) may not demonstrate a crowded field; many or “voluminous” examples (the opinion cites prior cases treating 14 or 26 examples as significant) can support a finding of weakness. The Board must explain why the number it finds is or is not “considerable.” (Opinion § III.B, citing Jack Wolfskin and Juice Generation.)
- Consumer education: If third‑party use is widespread and in overlapping channels, it supports the conclusion that consumers are accustomed to distinguishing similar marks — i.e., the mark has narrower protection and a different commercial impression (less likely to indicate single source).
- Nature of the marks: Similarity in formative word or distinctive elements matters. The opinion addressed “Aspire” and “Aspire‑formative” marks across financial services; the Board should explain which elements of the third‑party marks are probative of strength/weakness.

How the Board should articulate the interplay on remand
- Use a consistent definition of the relevant services/public. Because the Board found the parties’ services “highly similar” in factor two, it must use that same scope in factor six (the court explicitly instructs this). (Opinion § III.B.)
- List and justify the universe of third‑party marks considered. Identify which marks were included or excluded and why (service overlap, nature of use, geographic/consumer channels, bona fide use vs. registrations only).
- Explain both quantity and quality. Say whether the number of similar marks and the nature of their use is sufficient to support a finding of weakness, citing the standard and precedent and explaining how the examples compare to past “considerable” or “voluminous” proofs.
- Connect strength to commercial impression. If the sixth‑factor reassessment changes the Board’s view of CC Serve’s mark as relatively weak or strong, explain concretely how that change affects the first‑factor analysis of appearance, sound, connotation, and—critically—commercial impression and likelihood of confusion. The Board should state whether the apparent similarity between marks is mitigated (or amplified) by the field’s crowdedness and why.
- Make evidentiary findings with reasons. The Board must tie each factual finding to the record (what exhibits show, whether use is for credit cards vs. broader banking, etc.) and then state the legal conclusion about the weight to assign in the DuPont calculus.

Bottom line
The panel’s vacation of the first factor flows from settled DuPont reasoning: third‑party use (sixth factor) is probative of a mark’s commercial strength, and strength is part of the commercial impression the first factor measures. When the Board’s sixth‑factor analysis may materially change the mark’s perceived commercial strength, the Board should reexamine and explain the first factor in light of that revised assessment — using the same scope of services, listing the third‑party marks considered, and tying the strength finding to a clear explanation of how the commercial impression (and thus likelihood of confusion) is affected. The Apex opinion instructs precisely that approach.

### Are there any First Amendment concerns with a § 2(d) likelihood-of-confusion refusal, and why didn’t the court address them?
Question:
Recent Supreme Court cases (Tam, Brunetti) raised constitutional issues for other Lanham Act bars. The court here treats § 2(d) as routine without discussing speech. Is the reason that § 2(d) is viewpoint-neutral and tied to source identification (thus permissible), or because registration denials do not restrict speech? In what scenarios, if any, would a § 2(d) refusal raise constitutional problems the Federal Circuit would need to analyze?
Answer:
Short answer: Yes, § 2(d) refusals can implicate the First Amendment in principle, but the Federal Circuit had no occasion to analyze the issue in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. because the dispute presented routine likelihood‑of‑confusion (DuPont) questions and the Board’s legal/factual errors were dispositive. The opinion treats § 2(d) as an ordinary, viewpoint‑neutral source‑identification inquiry and confines the appeal to whether the Board correctly applied the DuPont factors, so the court did not reach any constitutional question.

Why the court stayed silent here
- The opinion frames the case as a straight § 2(d) (likelihood of confusion) appeal: “Likelihood of confusion is a question of law, based on findings of relevant underlying facts, namely findings under the DuPont factors.” The court’s mandate was to test the Board’s DuPont findings for legal error and substantial evidence. Because the parties’ briefing and the Board’s action centered on DuPont factor analysis (factors 1, 2 and 6), the Federal Circuit resolved those trademark questions and remanded for further factfinding where appropriate. In short, there was no need, on the issues presented, to reach First Amendment concerns.

Why that is legally plausible
- § 2(d) is traditionally treated as a source‑identification rule: the DuPont framework asks whether consumer confusion as to source is likely. The Apex opinion repeatedly ties the dispute to those ordinary trademark questions (similarity of marks, similarity of goods/services, third‑party uses, commercial strength). Because the Board’s refusal was analyzed under those neutral, source‑identifying principles, the court had a principled basis to decide the case without invoking free‑speech doctrine.
- The opinion’s outcome (vacating and remanding certain DuPont findings) shows the court viewed the controversy as one of trademark law factfinding and legal application rather than a constitutional challenge that would require a different analytical apparatus.

When a § 2(d) refusal would force a First Amendment analysis
The Federal Circuit would need to address the First Amendment only if the way § 2(d) was applied turned the matter from a neutral, source‑identification inquiry into a content‑ or viewpoint‑based penalty on speech or imposed a non‑trivial burden on expressive activity. Examples (fact patterns the court would likely have to confront) include:
- Viewpoint discrimination: if the PTO/Board denied registration because of the message conveyed by the mark (favoring some viewpoints over others) rather than because of traditional source‑confusion concerns, that would raise the same kind of constitutional problem the Supreme Court found in other Lanham Act provisions that discriminated by viewpoint.
- Content‑based suppression of expressive marks: if a mark is primarily expressive (e.g., a political slogan, advocacy message, or artistic expression) and the Board’s § 2(d) analysis treats the expressive content as the reason for refusal (not the likelihood of source confusion), the court would have to determine whether the government’s action is a content‑based restriction and, if so, what level of scrutiny applies.
- A meaningful burden on expression tied to registration: registration is a government benefit, but in some contexts registration (and its attendant advantages) may be functionally necessary to participate in particular forums or markets. If denial of registration effectively foreclosed the applicant’s ability to speak in a way that cannot be justified by classic source‑confusion concerns, constitutional review could be required.

Bottom line
Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. stays within the familiar DuPont framework and resolves statutory trademark questions. The Federal Circuit’s silence on the First Amendment reflects that posture: the panel corrected and remanded the Board’s likelihood‑of‑confusion findings without needing to reach constitutional issues. Only when a § 2(d) denial departs from neutral source‑identification analysis and operates as viewpoint‑ or content‑based suppression (or imposes a substantial burden on protected expression) would the court be compelled to engage First Amendment analysis.

### Why were costs awarded to the appellant despite only a partial victory on appeal?
Question:
The judgment states “Costs to appellant,” even though the court affirmed factor two and only vacated factors six and one. Under Fed. R. App. P. 39(a)(4) and Federal Circuit Rule 39, how does the court determine the prevailing party when the result is affirmed-in-part, vacated-in-part, and remanded? What principles guide awarding costs in mixed outcomes like this?
Answer:
Short answer: Because Rule 39(a)(4) leaves allocation of costs to the court when an appeal is affirmed-in-part and vacated-in-part, the Federal Circuit exercised that discretion here and treated Apex as the “prevailing party” for purposes of appellate costs. The opinion shows why: the court affirmed only one DuPont factor but vacated two others and remanded for further proceedings — a substantive change in the Board’s disposition — and the court therefore ordered “Costs to appellant.”

Explanation and legal basis

- The controlling rule. Fed. R. App. P. 39(a)(4) (as reflected in the Federal Circuit’s rules and practice) provides that when an appeal is “affirmed in part, reversed in part, modified, or vacated,” costs “are taxed only as the court orders.” That is, in mixed outcomes the court has discretion to allocate costs rather than being bound automatically to tax them to appellant or appellee.

- What the court did in this opinion. The panel expressly affirmed the Board’s finding on the second DuPont factor, but it “vacate[d] the Board’s finding with respect to the sixth DuPont factor and remand[ed] for reconsideration” and likewise remanded the first factor (because reconsideration of factor six could affect commercial strength and overall commercial impression). See opinion, Sections III.A–C. The judgment concludes: “AFFIRMED-IN-PART, VACATED-IN-PART, AND REMANDED” and then plainly: “COSTS — Costs to appellant.”

- Principles that guide the discretionary award in mixed outcomes. Where Rule 39 gives the court a choice, appellate courts generally allocate costs to reflect which party obtained the consequential relief on appeal. Relevant considerations (all implicit in the court’s exercise of discretion here) include:
  - whether the appellant obtained meaningful relief (reversal or vacatur of dispositive findings or remand for reconsideration that alters the Board’s outcome),
  - whether the appeal changed the legal or factual posture in a way that vindicates the appellant’s principal objectives,
  - whether one party prevailed on the issues most central to the dispute rather than only on peripheral points,
  - and practical fairness (e.g., whether one party substantially prevailed overall).

Applied to Apex Bank v. CC Serve: although the panel left one DuPont factor intact, it vacated two of the Board’s factor findings (six and one) and remanded for reconsideration in light of the Board’s own finding that the parties’ services are highly similar. That vacatur/remand is more than a narrow technical correction — it requires the Board to revisit the strength and commercial impression of the registered mark and could alter the ultimate registration outcome. The court therefore exercised the Rule 39(a)(4) discretion to treat Apex as the prevailing party for purposes of taxing appellate costs and entered the single-line award “Costs to appellant.”

Takeaway for similar appeals: when an appeal produces mixed results, you cannot assume costs will automatically be taxed to the loser; Rule 39 gives the court discretion, and courts will often award costs to the party that achieved the substantial or dispositive relief on appeal (including vacatur/remand that meaningfully advances that party’s position).

### Does the TTAB receive any deference on how it applies the DuPont framework, or are those legal judgments reviewed de novo?
Question:
The opinion reviews legal conclusions de novo and factual findings for substantial evidence, and corrects the Board’s legal framing of factor six. Is there any Chevron or Skidmore deference owed to the PTO/TTAB in interpreting or structuring the DuPont factors, or is the multi-factor framework purely judicial and therefore policed de novo by the Federal Circuit?
Answer:
Short answer
No — at least as applied in this opinion, the Federal Circuit gave no Chevron- or Skidmore-type deference to the TTAB’s legal framing of the DuPont analysis. The court reiterated that it reviews the Board’s legal conclusions de novo and will correct legal errors in how the Board defines or applies the DuPont factors, while leaving factual findings (e.g., scope of services, existence/extent of third‑party use) to substantial‑evidence review.

Why (grounded in the opinion)
- The court starts by stating the controlling standard: “We review the [B]oard’s legal conclusions de novo, and its findings of fact for substantial evidence.” (Section II (citing M2 Software)). It then reiterates that “Likelihood of confusion is a question of law, based on findings of relevant underlying facts, namely findings under the DuPont factors.” (Id.).
- The court treated the Board’s treatment of the sixth DuPont factor as a legal error. It explains that the Board “restricted the universe of marks it considered” in a way that conflicted with its prior factual finding that the parties’ services were “highly similar,” and says explicitly “That was an error.” (Section III.B). The court vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor finding and remanded for reconsideration under the correct legal framing.
- The court also vacated the first‑factor analysis for reconsideration because the legal error on factor six could alter the mark’s commercial strength and therefore the legal analysis of “appearance, sound, connotation and commercial impression.” (Section III.C).

What this means in practice
- Legal questions about how to frame or apply DuPont (e.g., what legal standard governs similarity under a factor, whether the Board may narrow the universe of comparable marks) are reviewed de novo by the Federal Circuit and are subject to correction when they conflict with the court’s legal standards.
- Factual determinations underlying those questions (e.g., the Board’s finding that the parties’ services are “highly similar,” the number and nature of third‑party uses) remain reviewable only for substantial evidence.

Caveat
The opinion does not discuss Chevron or Skidmore by name; it resolves the matter by applying the familiar Federal Circuit standard—de novo review of legal conclusions and substantial‑evidence review of facts—and by correcting the Board’s legal framing. So, at least as to the DuPont framework in this decision, the Board’s legal structuring is not entitled to agency‑style deference.

### On remand, is the Board bound by the Federal Circuit’s factor-two finding, or can it revisit it under the mandate rule?
Question:
The court affirmed the Board’s analysis of the second DuPont factor but vacated and remanded as to factors six and one. Under the mandate rule/law-of-the-case, is the TTAB precluded from reopening factor two or other affirmed findings, and must it limit its reconsideration to the scope-of-similarity under factor six and the resulting commercial impression under factor one?
Answer:
Short answer: The Board is bound by the Federal Circuit’s affirmed finding on the second DuPont factor and may not relitigate that factual determination on remand absent new evidence or changed circumstances. The Board must use that finding (that the parties’ services are “highly similar” and partially legally identical) as the starting point when reexamining factors six and one, and it must expand its inquiry under factor six to include third‑party “Aspire” uses for the broader banking/finance services that the court said are within the scope of similarity.

Why: the opinion’s directives and the governing standard

- The court expressly “affirm[ed] the Board’s finding as to the second DuPont factor” and stated that “substantial evidence supports the Board’s finding that the parties’ services are highly similar.” That factual finding was therefore decided on appeal. The opinion also explains the applicable review standard: “[w]e review the [B]oard’s legal conclusions de novo, and its findings of fact for substantial evidence.” Because the panel affirmed the Board’s factual finding under that standard, the Board must accept that factual finding on remand unless there is a new factual basis that would justify revisiting it.

- The court vacated only factors six and one and remanded for reconsideration “in light of its finding that the parties’ services are highly similar.” The opinion squarely instructs the Board that “when the Board has already made a factual finding that the services are highly similar … the Board should retain the same scope in its consideration of similarity under the other factors.” That is a clear mandate about the scope and frame of the remand.

What the Board may and must do on remand

- Must accept the affirmed second‑factor finding: The Board should not reopen or redecide the second DuPont factor as part of this remand. The court’s affirmance is the law of the case for this factual issue; the Board’s further analyses must proceed against that backdrop.

- Must expand the universe of third‑party marks for factor six: Because the court found the Board erred by restricting factor six to credit‑card uses only and instructed the Board to retain the same scope used in its factor‑two analysis, the Board must consider third‑party “Aspire” (and Aspire‑formative) uses across the related banking and finance services the court found similar. The Board should reassess the number and nature of similar marks with that broader scope.

- Must reexamine the overall commercial impression for factor one in light of the adjusted factor‑six analysis: The opinion vacated factor one because the court concluded that a different assessment of commercial strength under factor six “may result in a different determination of the mark’s commercial strength or weakness and affect the overall commercial impression.” The Board therefore must redetermine the marks’ appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression against the affirmed factual backdrop.

When the Board may revisit factor two

- The mandate rule and law‑of‑the‑case doctrine do not absolutely freeze the record against any possible new development. If, on remand, new evidence is properly introduced (for example, evidence that did not exist or was unavailable at the time of the original proceedings) or if the Board identifies a clearly articulated basis showing the prior factual finding is no longer supported, the Board may justify reconsidering the second factor — but it must explain why that evidence or changed circumstance warrants departing from the appellate court’s prior affirmance. The opinion itself gives no reason to reopen factor two; it directs the Board to proceed with factor six and factor one in light of the affirmed second‑factor finding.

Bottom line: The Board is constrained by the Federal Circuit’s affirmed finding on factor two and must apply that finding when redoing factors six and one. It should broaden the factor‑six inquiry to include banking/finance uses the court said are similar and then reassess commercial impression under factor one. Reopening factor two is permissible only if new, material evidence or circumstances justify it and the Board explains why that evidence overcomes the appellate court’s prior substantial‑evidence determination.

